Fillets: a rounding whose end is cut by several faces around a corner
patch of a cylinder (mitcad#133).

The case: the corner edges of a hexagonal frustum rounded, each edge ending
at a vertex where a cylinder cuts the frustum's corner, under flat slivers
between the frustum's top edges and the cylinder. The contact lines reach
the slivers beyond the vertex, so ChFi3d_Builder::PerformIntersectionAtEnd
cuts the rounding with a sliver, the cylinder and the next sliver. Four
things went wrong there:

- The fillet's crossing with the common edge of two faces at the end was
  the one nearest to the vertex on the edge's whole curve. The slivers'
  edges with the cylinder are arcs of one circle, so both edges got the
  same crossing, the cylinder's part of the end was empty and the trimmed
  surface threw (Geom_RectangularTrimmedSurface V1==V2). The crossings on
  the edge come first now, then the one nearest to the edge's range.
- That crossing lies on the arc's extension beyond its end vertex. As where
  the edge is not crossed at all (the faces' surfaces intersected and the
  common boundary prolonged from the vertex), the common boundary is now
  prolonged along the edge's own curve from that end vertex (the faces keep
  their surfaces), and the edge of the corner patch that runs from there to
  the vertex (or to the end of an edge prolonged before) is cut at that
  end, so that the corner patch goes with the corner. Not so where the
  fillet passes beside a face between the end's first and last faces (a
  narrow chamfer at a corner of a step): when the crossing with that
  face's first edge lies beyond the corner where the previous edge meets
  it and outside both faces of the edge, and so does the crossing with its
  next edge, the crossings are taken as before (the prolongation would
  bound neither face and gave a self-intersecting wire).
- At the corner the cylinder's seam runs from, the seam was counted as an
  edge of the vertex, which sent the corner to the search for faces around
  a vertex of more than three edges ("Failed to find edge"). When the seam
  lies only on a face at the end and both contacts end on edges away from
  the vertex, it is not counted; the faces at the end are found along the
  fillet's faces as at the other corners.
- On that cylinder the two ends of its part of the end come from the
  p-curves of edges on the two sides of the seam, in different periods:
  they are taken the nearer way across the seam, the intersection is split
  where it crosses the seam (each piece with its own p-curve) and the seam
  is cut there, as PerformOneCorner does.
